# Page-turn grammar

## Purpose

Use the physical book as a storytelling device: what the reader sees at
once on two facing pages, what the turn of a page hides and reveals, and how
many pages a bound comic can have.

## Scope

Applies only when `comic_format` in `production/project.yaml` is `paged`.
Governs the `side`, `spread_with`, and `page_turn` fields of every
`page.yaml`, and the page budgets in the series plan. Sits on top of
`rules/common/` and the domain layers, and narrows `layout/pacing.md` for
bound pages without contradicting it.

## Core Principles

- Facing pages are seen together. The reader's eye catches the whole
  opening before reading a word, so nothing on it is a surprise.
- The page turn is the only curtain a comic has. Surprises go behind it.
- A page's position in the book is part of its plan. Moving a page from a
  right-hand to a left-hand position changes what it can do.
- The page count belongs to the binding as much as to the story.

## Left and right pages

- Every page declares `side`: `left`, `right`, or `single`.
- In a book read `ltr`, page 1 is a right-hand page. Odd pages fall on the
  right and even pages on the left. In a book read `rtl` the binding is
  mirrored: page 1 is a left-hand page, odd pages fall on the left and even
  pages on the right.
- `single` is for paged projects delivered one page at a time (a PDF read in
  single-page view, a page-per-swipe reader). Every page boundary is then a
  turn, and the constraints on facing pages do not apply.
- A project with a `print` deliverable uses `left` and `right`. If the same
  pages are also read on screens, plan for the book: a layout that works
  across a turn also works on a swipe.
- When a page is inserted or removed, every later page changes side. Re-run
  `/page-plan` for the rest of the issue and re-check every reveal.

## What the turn does

`page_turn` states what the move from this page to the next one does.

| Value | Meaning | Where it belongs |
|---|---|---|
| `none` | The page ends, and the next one starts fresh | Any page; the usual value before a facing page |
| `continuation` | An action or a line carries over the boundary | Any page |
| `reveal` | The next page opens on something the reader must not see early | The page before a turn |
| `cliffhanger` | The page ends on an open question or a threat | The page before a turn, and the last page of an issue |

- In an `ltr` book the turn comes after a right-hand page. A reveal is
  therefore set up in the last panel of a right-hand page and delivered in
  the first panel of the left-hand page that follows. In `rtl` the sides are
  swapped.
- A `reveal` or a `cliffhanger` SHOULD NOT be set on a page whose next page
  faces it. The reader has already seen the answer across the fold.
- The panel that delivers a reveal is the first in its page's reading order
  and SHOULD be the largest panel of that page.
- The last panel before a turn asks a question the drawing can pose: a look
  off panel, a door opening, a sound. It does not answer it.
- Do not spend the turn on nothing. A right-hand page (in `ltr`) that ends a
  scene quietly is fine, but a run of turns with `none` is a sign that
  reveals are landing mid-opening where they are wasted.

## Double-page spreads

- A spread is two facing pages composed as one image. Each page names the
  other in `spread_with`, and each keeps its own `page.yaml`, render, and
  review.
- In `ltr` a spread is an even-numbered left page followed by the next odd
  right page. Two pages on either side of a turn cannot form a spread.
- `layout.grid` on both pages describes the shared composition, and the
  panel that crosses the fold is recorded in the `layout.notes` of each.
  Reading order runs across the full width of a tier before dropping to the
  next.
- The two halves are reviewed side by side. Each review lists the other
  half's render under `compared_against.neighbors`, and the meeting edge is
  inspected for line, tone, and alignment.
- Faces, hands, and lettering stay out of the fold (see `print-specs.md`).
- A spread costs two pages of the budget. Default: one spread in an issue of
  20 to 24 pages, reserved for the largest image the issue has.

## Page count conventions

- A saddle-stitched booklet is made of folded sheets, so its total page
  count, covers and blank pages included, is a multiple of 4. A book bound
  in signatures is planned in multiples of 8 or 16 according to the
  printer.
- Common story lengths: 20 to 24 pages for a single issue and 8 to 12 for a
  short. These are defaults, and `page_budget` in the series plan is the
  project's actual number.
- The story starts on a right-hand page in `ltr`. Chapter openings in a
  longer book SHOULD also fall on the right, with a blank or a title page
  inserted when they would not.
- Decide what fills the remaining pages of the sheet count (title page,
  credits, back matter) when the budget is set. The series plan's notes
  record it.
- Projects with no `print` deliverable are free of the multiple-of-4 rule
  and still benefit from an even count, since readers and reader apps show
  pages in pairs.

## Webtoon techniques out of place

- Do not pace a paged comic by gutter length alone. A tall empty gap costs a
  third of a printed page and reads as a layout fault.
- Do not plan a panel taller than the page. Vertical reveals that depend on
  scrolling belong to `rules/webtoon/`.
- Converting between formats is a new layout. Pages are re-planned for the
  other format and never sliced or stacked mechanically.

## Validation

- `schemas/page.schema.json` restricts `side` to `left`, `right`, or
  `single`, `page_turn` to its four values, and `spread_with` to a page ID
  or `null`.
- `scripts/validate-workspace.js` schema-checks every page file and reports
  an issue whose `page_count` differs from the page directories on disk.
- The agreement of `side` with page number and reading direction, the
  pairing of `spread_with` on both halves, and the placement of reveals
  against the turn are read from the page files by the layout-artist in the
  self-review of `/page-plan`. `scripts/build-board.js` lays the issue's
  pages out in order for that check.
- Page-count multiples depend on the binding. The prepress-specialist
  confirms them against the printer's specification before a `print`
  deliverable is prepared.
- Whether a turn works is human review on the thumbnails, read in pairs as
  the book will be.
